{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789033491,"nonce":"NG6FscbkvOOSrzVoSTe8UiqVIdE=","message":"Measured against the committed C reference (crates/ash/test/tests/Mandelbrot_reference.c), clang 17, arm64. Both mandelbrot sizes, AOT for the whole-program LLVM answer.\n\n  arm                          small(298^2)   full(875x500)\n  clang -ffp-contract=off        22816350       112790102\n  clang default (=on)            22825041       112798515\n  clang -ffp-contract=fast       22825041       112798802\n  ash interp                     22816350       112790102\n  ash cranelift                  22816350       112790102\n  ash AOT (shipped)              22825041       112798637\n  ash AOT, peephole off          22816350       112798802\n  ash AOT, no fusion at all      22816350       112790102\n\nThree things this settles.\n\n1. ash's LLVM policy IS clang's 'fast', not its default. With the AIR peephole off, the blanket 'contract' FMF on every float op reproduces -ffp-contract=fast BIT-EXACTLY (112798802). clang's default instead emits 4 explicit llvm.fmuladd from the front end and sets NO contract flags -- fusion is chosen before any optimisation, by expression. ash sets the flag everywhere and lets the backend choose. That is the mechanism difference, and it is why the two land on different sites.\n\n2. One divergence was ours and is fixed (6beefd3). ASH_FMA_WHY showed 5 of 7 refusals were 'operand shares the product register': the peephole reused the register the deleted product freed for the negation, so it refused whenever a multiplicand lived there. Giving the negation a fresh register makes 'x * scale - 2.5' fuse like 'y * scale - 1' already did, and the SMALL mandelbrot then matches clang's default bit-exactly.\n\n3. The residual on the full image is structural, not a tuning knob. One site still differs in form: clang computes fma(i, i, -(j*j)), ash computes fma(-j, j, i*i), because i*i is shared between complexLength2 and complexSquare and the peephole requires a single-use product. That requirement is CORRECT and there is a test asserting it -- C agrees: 't = a*b; x = t + c;' does not contract either, since contraction is per-expression. clang never shares because it fuses before CSE. HL bytecode reaches ash with expressions already flattened, so the provenance that decision needs is gone before AIR exists. See 907a492.\n\nTested and refuted on the way: moving FmaPeephole before LICM, before GVN, and before the inliner each changed NOTHING; so did removing 'inline' from the Haxe source. Allowing multi-use products to fuse moved ash AWAY from clang (it is 'fast' semantics) and broke the invariant test.\n\nConsequence for this issue: 'fuse by construction so every engine agrees' is reachable, but the target cannot be clang's default. The only setting ash matches bit-exactly on BOTH sizes is -ffp-contract=off -- which is already what interp and Cranelift produce, so agreement-by-construction means choosing between exact parity (no fusion) and fusion (what hxcpp/hxjava do). That is a policy call, not a bug.\n\nNot changed: the blanket contract flag is still on by default. Gating it off was measured (no timing difference on mandelbrot AOT, 5 runs, macOS) but one benchmark on the wrong machine decides nothing -- see the standing rule about the NUC.","files":null}]}